WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.289
Welcome to CBE STEM Academy, Grade Seven Integrated Science. Today we learn about the male reproductive system.

00:00:10.289 --> 00:00:20.000
We will name its main parts, explain their jobs, and follow the route taken by sperm.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:31.211
This side-view cutaway shows parts usually hidden inside the body. Some organs are inside the pelvis. Others are outside.

00:00:31.211 --> 00:00:45.000
Find the testis, scrotum, sperm duct, glands, penis, and urethra. The bladder helps us locate the organs. It belongs to the urinary system.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:57.736
There are usually two testes. One is called a testis. They make sperm, the male reproductive cells. Sperm production normally starts during puberty.

00:00:57.736 --> 00:01:10.000
Sperm develop inside tiny, coiled tubes. The testes also make testosterone. This hormone is a chemical messenger that helps control body changes.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.420
The scrotum is a skin pouch that holds the testes outside the main body cavity. Sperm production works best slightly below normal body temperature.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:30.000
Muscles move the testes closer to the body in cold conditions and farther away in warm conditions. This helps control temperature.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.062
Beside each testis is a tightly coiled tube called the epididymis. Sperm move from the testis into this tube.

00:01:39.062 --> 00:01:55.000
Here, sperm continue to mature and are stored for a time. Remember: the testis makes sperm. The epididymis helps them mature and stores them. The enlarged picture shows its coils.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:06.060
The sperm duct carries sperm away from the epididymis. Its other name is the vas deferens. Each testis has its own duct.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:20.000
This duct travels up into the pelvis and around the bladder. Muscles in its wall squeeze to move sperm along. Watch the dots show the direction.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:30.907
Glands are small organs that make and release substances. The seminal vesicles lie behind the bladder. They add fluid containing sugar that supplies energy to sperm.

00:02:30.907 --> 00:02:39.407
The prostate is just below the bladder. It also adds fluid. Other small glands add a slippery fluid to the urethra.

00:02:39.407 --> 00:02:50.000
Sperm mixed with these fluids is called semen. Sperm are cells; semen is the mixture that carries them. The bladder stores urine, not semen.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.148
The penis is an external organ. A tube called the urethra runs through it and opens at the tip.

00:02:58.148 --> 00:03:15.000
Urine and semen both leave through the urethra, but normally at different times. During ejaculation, a muscle at the bladder opening helps stop urine and semen from mixing.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.493
Follow the pathway. Sperm are made in the testes. They move into the epididymis to mature and be stored.

00:03:23.493 --> 00:03:35.606
Next, sperm travel along the sperm duct. Fluid from a seminal vesicle joins the pathway. A short joining tube passes through the prostate into the urethra.

00:03:35.606 --> 00:03:45.569
Glands add more fluid. Semen passes through the urethra and leaves at the tip of the penis. This release is called ejaculation.

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:55.000
Sperm do not enter the bladder. Muscles help move them through the ducts. The dots are enlarged and the movement is speeded up.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:06.003
A sperm cell is tiny. We need a microscope to see it clearly. Its head carries genetic information for inherited features.

00:04:06.003 --> 00:04:20.000
The middle piece releases energy. The tail helps it swim. During fertilisation, a sperm joins with an egg cell. We will explore that process in another lesson.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:30.501
During puberty, the organs develop and sperm production begins. A boy may notice a deeper voice and more body hair. People develop at different times.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:40.000
Keep the outside of the genital area clean. Ask a trusted adult or health worker about worrying changes. Never tease someone about their body.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:48.066
Pause and answer. Which organs make sperm? Where do sperm mature? What is semen?

00:04:48.066 --> 00:05:00.000
Check: the testes make sperm. Sperm mature in the epididymis. Semen is sperm mixed with fluids from glands. Review the pathway if you need to.
